 "title": "3 Things Never to Do in a Frame Attack",
 "topic": "mindset",
 "summary": "Chase Hughes explains the three things you should never do during a frame attack. The main lesson is to avoid self-justification, defending your motives, or arguing character questions because these are traps designed to label you.",
 "key_points": [
  "Never explain yourself under pressure, as doing so submits to a frame and forces you to justify yourself.",
  "Never argue about your motive because debating your intent means they have already redefined your identity.",
  "Never take bait on character questions, as 100 percent of character questions are traps meant to label you, not understand you.",
  "Always give them a bridge to retreat by saying phrases like 'maybe you didn't mean to'."
 ],
 "content": "Instagram reel/post by Chase Hughes | Behavior Expert. Text / caption: Comment “FREE” and Chase’s free human behavior training will be sent to you. Chase Hughes teaches human behavior, influence, and behavioral profiling built from two decades of military and intelligence experience. Follow @chasehughesclips for more. Make a note of this post. Transcript: The 10 things you should permanently delete and never do for the rest of your life in a frame attack. Ever. Never ever ever. Number one. Never explain yourself under pressure. If you explain under pressure you are submitting to a frame. Think about the phrase, you know, I don't think you meant to, but that question is designed to put me in a position of justifying myself and that's not a role that I'm taking on right now. Number two. Never ever argue about your motive. If you're debating your own intent, they have already redefined your identity. So for this one think about the phrase, you know, that maybe you didn't intend to, but that comment puts me in a spot where I'm defending what kind of person I am and I'm not stepping into that box. Number three. Never take debate on character questions. This is the biggest one that you'll hear from narcissists. All of them are traps. 100% of character questions are traps. The goal is not to understand you, it's to label you. Think about the phrase, framing me like that forces me to argue about who I am. I can see the play and I'm not stepping into it and I'm not saying you did that on purpose. Always give them the bridge to retreat right? So that's why we say maybe you didn't mean to. I don't think you meant to do it. Text on screen: Never Do These 3 Things WHEN YOU'RE ATTACKED, 10 IN A FRAME ATTACK, NUMBER ONE, 1 NEVER EXPLAIN YOURSELF UNDER PRESSURE, NUMBER 2, 2 NEVER ARGUE ABOUT YOUR MOTIVE, NUMBER 3, 3 NEVER TAKE THE BAIT ON CHARACTER QUESTIONS",
